Finding: {"title":"syncInvoiceToQuickBooks / syncPaymentToQuickBooks call qbConnectionRepo.findActive() as a gate but then use a disconnected legacy user-scoped token store","severity":"MEDIUM","area":"architecture / dead-code-and-drift","files":["src/lib/quickbooks-sync.ts","src/lib/quickbooks.ts","src/repositories/quickbooks/qbConnectionRepo.ts"],"detail":"`syncInvoiceToQuickBooks` (src/lib/quickbooks-sync.ts:205) and `syncPaymentToQuickBooks` (line 351) both call `await qbConnectionRepo.findActive(invoice.flightSchoolId)` — which throws `QBNotConnectedError` if the school has no active row in the newer, school-scoped `qb_connections` table — but never use the returned connection object. Both functions then call `QuickBooksClient.create(qbUserId)` (src/lib/quickbooks.ts:153), which reads tokens from the entirely separate, legacy, per-*user* `userIntegrations` table. The `findActive()` call functions only as a gate while the actual API calls run against a different, disconnected credential store scoped by user rather than by school.","impact":"A school could pass the `findActive` gate (active `qb_connections` row) yet have no `userIntegrations` row for the given `qbUserId`, causing `QuickBooksClient.create` to throw a confusing 'No QuickBooks integration found for user' error that masks the real cause. The two-token-store split (confirmed by `LEGACY_QB_REPLACEMENT` messaging and `ENABLE_LEGACY_USER_SCOPED_QB_SYNC` flag in routes-quickbooks.ts) is a known-in-progress migration, but `quickbooks-sync.ts` layers the new gate on top of the old token flow without reconciling them.","recommendation":"Either (a) make these functions actually consume the `QbConnection` row returned by `findActive()` to build the QB client, fully migrating off `userIntegrations`, or (b) if superseded by `qbInvoiceSyncService`/`qbPaymentSyncService` (referenced in routes-quickbooks.ts as the new path), delete the unused `findActive()` call and clearly mark `quickbooks-sync.ts` as legacy-only.","confidence":"MEDIUM"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

